1994:{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-06-12T00:13:11.170Z","sessionId":"80b51e31-b580-43b6-8244-fd7633ee1338","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9583582925c26324</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01J3jox4iZtSvxhRtEdSoJHv</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-0/-/80b51e31-b580-43b6-8244-fd7633ee1338/tasks/a9583582925c26324.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Scope verification gauntlet (read-only)\" completed</summary>\n<result>I have everything. The `redux.sync` is emitted as inline PTX asm in `primitives.cuh:196` (becomes `REDUX` in SASS, but profile_maximal does NOT grep it). All four audit findings, the maximality check, the race harness, and the ordering/budget are fully mapped. Let me compile the surgical execution plan.\n\n---\n\n# VERIFICATION GAUNTLET — EXECUTION PLAN (33 L3-TC wgmma cells)\n\nAll paths absolute. Run after the builder's rebuild lands and `import grokking_optimizers._ops` succeeds. Cells = 11 opts × 3 models {decoder, vit, mamba}.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 — MAX-PTX maximality check\n\n**Script:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_optimizers/profile_maximal.py`\n\n**CRITICAL CORRECTION up front:** this tool audits **SASS** (disassembled cubin), **not PTX**, and it does **NOT** profile the 33 mega cells individually. It compiles **4 GEMM-bearing model TUs** (Tier A) + a **4-cell sample** (Tier B), because the \"maximal\" decision is in the compute-bound model GEMMs — the 33 element-local optimizer tails are scalar by design (`profile_maximal.py:303-312`, `:366-370`). There is no per-cell-×33 loop in this tool.\n\n### Invocations\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\n# Full SASS audit (Tiers A+B need nvcc+cuobjdump; ~25 min, compile-heavy):\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 -m grokking_optimizers.profile_maximal 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/pmax.log\n# Tier A only (the WGMMA/TMA/spill maximality decision, 4 model TUs):\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 -m grokking_optimizers.profile_maximal --tier A 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/pmax_A.log\n# Tier B only (ptxas regs/smem/spills on a 4-cell sample + 3 opt kernels):\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 -m grokking_optimizers.profile_maximal --tier B 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/pmax_B.log\n```\nNo flag selects an arbitrary cell. The Tier-B sample is hardcoded at `profile_maximal.py:367-371`: `mamba3/adamw, mamba3/supergrok2, transformer_decoder/muon, vit/prodigy` + opt kernels `launch_adamw, launch_supergrok2, launch_muon`. To audit a different cell's SASS you must edit `_CELL_SAMPLE` (you said read-only — so accept this sample as the representative set, OR run the per-cell wgmma proof through `wiring_check.py` in Section 2 instead, which observes the live engine).\n\n### How it gets the cubin (your Q)\n`_nvcc_profile()` at `profile_maximal.py:258-296`: it compiles the `.cu` itself with `nvcc -o &lt;tmp&gt;.o` (`:261-262`, flags `_MAX_NVCC` at `:153-158` = `-O3 --use_fast_math -DNDEBUG arch=compute_90a,code=sm_90a`, plus `-Xptxas -v`), then runs **`cuobjdump -sass &lt;tmp&gt;.o`** (`:285`). So: **SASS via cuobjdump**, on a freshly-compiled object — **not** nvdisasm, **not** PTX, **not** a prebuilt cubin.\n\n### PASS criterion (literal strings to grep in the log)\nPer-TU PASS gate is `pr.wgmma&gt;0 and pr.tma&gt;0 and pr.c7509==0 and pr.unexpected_spill_bytes==0` (`:337-338`). In the printed log:\n\n| Check | PASS signal | FAIL signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overall TU | `PASS  [A] &lt;label&gt;` | `FAIL  [A] &lt;label&gt;` |\n| (a) WGMMA present | `WGMMA=&lt;N&gt;` with N&gt;0 in the detail line | `← NO tensor-core MMA (not maximal!)` (`:343`) |\n| (a) TMA present | `TMA=&lt;N&gt;` with N&gt;0 | (TMA=0 → overall FAIL, no dedicated banner) |\n| not serialized | `C7509=0` | `← wgmma SERIALIZED (not maximal!)` (`:345`) |\n| (b) zero spills | `spills=0B` (or `spills=8B` **with** the dead-code note) | `← register SPILLS (not maximal!)` (`:347`) |\n| Final verdict | `VERDICT: PASS` (`:715`) | `VERDICT: FAIL — &lt;n&gt; probe(s) not maximal` (`:720`) |\n\n```bash\ngrep -E \"FAIL |VERDICT: FAIL|not maximal!|SERIALIZED\" /tmp/pmax_A.log   # any hit = fail\ngrep -E \"PASS .*\\[A\\]|VERDICT: PASS|WGMMA=[1-9]\" /tmp/pmax_A.log         # pass evidence\n```\n\n**The spill marker (your Q):** the literal ptxas string is **`bytes spill stores`** (regex `profile_maximal.py:255,268`). The per-kernel block is `Function properties for &lt;MANGLED&gt; ... N bytes stack frame, M bytes spill stores`. There is **exactly ONE allowlisted 8-byte spiller** — the dead-code CUTLASS TF32-RS GEMM `MMA_64x128x8_F32TF32TF32_RS_TN` (runtime-unreachable via `vit_run_gemm_atb&lt;float&gt;` scalar fallback; allowlist tokens at `:198-202`). So **`spills=8B` is still a PASS** iff the detail line also shows `ALLOWED: known dead-code CUTLASS TF32-RS GEMM`. Any OTHER spiller → `unexpected_spill_bytes&gt;0` → FAIL. This is confirmed live in `.audit_notes.md:84-90` (6P/5F where all 5 fails were this single 8B dead kernel).\n\n### (a) WGMMA + TMA, (c) fast-math — IMPORTANT naming gotcha\n- **WGMMA in SASS is `HGMMA`/`GMMA`**, not the literal \"wgmma\". The regex is `\\b[HUW]?GMMA\\b` (`:287`). **`grep wgmma` returns 0 = false-negative** — `.audit_notes.md:87` confirms: \"grep 'wgmma'=0 is false-neg (SASS mnemonic=HGMMA). 256 HGMMA + 622 TMA(UTMALDG)\". Grep the cubin for `HGMMA` and `UTMALDG`, not `wgmma`/`TMA`.\n- **TMA** = `UTMALDG|UBLKCP|cp.async.bulk` (`:288`).\n- **(c) inlined fast-math** — profile_maximal does **NOT** grep `rsqrt.approx`/`ex2.approx`/`fma.rn`/`redux.sync`. Those are **PTX**-level strings; at SASS level they lower to **`MUFU`** (rsqrt/ex2 → `MUFU.RSQ`/`MUFU.EX2`) and **`FFMA`** (fma.rn), which the tool *does* count and print as `MUFU=&lt;N&gt;` (`:290,339`). The PTX fast-math proof lives separately in **`/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/STAGE3_PTX_AUDIT.md`** (documents `ex2.approx.f32`:12,43; `rsqrt.approx.f32`:13,195; `fma.rn.f32`:158,211). `redux.sync.add.u32` is hand-written inline PTX asm at **`/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/csrc/backends/cuda/sm_90/primitives.cuh:196`** (not grepped by profile_maximal). So to check (c) you grep two different surfaces:\n```bash\n# SASS form (what profile_maximal prints): expect MUFU&gt;0 and FFMA&gt;0\ngrep -E \"MUFU=[1-9]|FFMA\" /tmp/pmax_A.log\n# raw PTX fast-math (if you compile -ptx yourself): the literal approx ops\n# documented in scripts/STAGE3_PTX_AUDIT.md — NOT produced by this tool\n```\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 — GROKKING RACE (multi-step functional test)\n\n**Harness:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_race_v2.py` (CLI: `:2493`). Selection: `--optimizers &lt;key&gt; --tasks &lt;model&gt;`; budget: `--early-stop-max-steps`; seeds: `--num-seeds`/`--seeds`.\n\n**KEY ARCHITECTURE FACT** (`.audit_notes.md:99-103`): the race runs **fused-OPTIMIZER kernels × EAGER PyTorch models**. The L3-TC megakernel path is taken inside `train_&lt;opt&gt;` via `_try_fused_train_step(mtype, opt, …)` (e.g. SG2 at `grokking_race_v2.py:1519`; SG11/15 at `:1259`/`:1385`; neuralgrok at `:1151`). To force the wgmma L3-TC engine you must set `matmul_precision=bf16` (the default) and `use_fused=True` (the default). **Also note `.audit_notes.md:101`: race `adamw` uses stock `torch.optim.AdamW(fused=True)`, NOT the pkg adamw kernel** — so adamw is not a kernel test.\n\n### Single-cell command (one optimizer × one model, L3-TC bf16)\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 grokking_race_v2.py \\\n  --optimizers supergrok2 --tasks decoder \\\n  --num-seeds 1 --train-test-ratios 50 \\\n  --early-stop-max-steps 15000 --eval-every 10 \\\n  --no-status-server 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/race_sg2_decoder.log\n```\n- Optimizer keys (`:51`, `wiring_check.py:51-52`): `adamw neuralgrok grokadamw supergrok supergrok15 supergrok2 grokfast muon lion looksam prodigy` (note SG11's race key is **`supergrok`**, not `supergrok11`).\n- Models: `decoder vit mamba`.\n- **GPU-time budget:** the documented decoder reference run was **15k steps** (`.audit_notes.md:113`, \"decoder 50/50 15k\"). A grokking cell crosses val≥0.95 and stops via patience (50 evals × eval_every 10 = 500-step hold, `:264-265`); a DNF runs the full `early_stop_max_steps`. Rough wall: decoder ~3-6 GPU-min/cell at 15k on H100; vit (MNIST, larger) ~2-3×; mamba (python-fallback scan, `.audit_notes.md:103`) slowest. Budget **15k steps; cap 20k** (the argparse default `:2511`).\n\n### Verify it actually took L3-TC (don't trust silently-degraded runs)\nThe train loop stamps `train_path` via the WIRING-GUARD (`grokking_race_v2.py:738-783`). A degrade prints to **stderr**:\n```bash\ngrep -E \"\\[WIRING-GUARD\\].*DEGRADED|expected .* but ran\" /tmp/race_sg2_decoder.log   # must be EMPTY\n```\nFor a pure path check without a full race, use the smoke variant (Section below).\n\n### GROK vs COLLAPSE vs HIDDEN-BUG signals (grep the log / result JSON)\nResults land in `results/&lt;output&gt;/...json` (per `TrainResult`, `:567-610`). The tqdm postfix prints `trn=… val=… tst=… tl=…` every eval (`:718`).\n\n| Outcome | Signal to grep |\n|---|---|\n| **GROK** | `val` and `tst` climb to ~`1.0` after a delay; result JSON `grokking_step_test_confirmed: true` + `final_test_acc` ≈ 1.0 + `stopping_reason: \"val_acc_threshold\"` (`:533`). |\n| **COLLAPSE (memorize-then-collapse)** | `trn` rises (e.g. 0.6-1.0) then **drops back** while `tst` stays ~chance; final `final_test_acc` ≈ `0.01` with a non-trivial `best_metric_acc` earlier. Documented pattern: `.audit_notes.md:153,171-172`. |\n| **HIDDEN BUG** | `tl=nan`/`tl=4.577` flat at `trn≈0.014` (chance = 1/97 ≈ 0.0103) for the whole run = NaN / no-learning; `component_failures` non-empty in JSON (`:606`) = a side-component (sam/meta/bilevel) silently broke; or a `[WIRING-GUARD] DEGRADED` line = parity/path drift. |\n\n```bash\ngrep -E \"nan|trn=0\\.01[0-4].*tl=4\\.5|component_failures|WIRING-GUARD\" /tmp/race_*.log\n```\n\n### EXPECTED to grok vs documented-WON'T-grok (with file:line citations)\nFrom the live run-1 result (`.audit_notes.md:113-118`) and the post-fix integration (`:168-178`):\n- **EXPECTED to GROK (8/11):** adamw, lion, grokfast, grokadamw, looksam, neuralgrok, muon, **prodigy** (prodigy now groks post-EMA-fix: `.audit_notes.md:169` \"Prodigy: GROKS @ step 1000\").\n- **Documented WON'T grok (memorize-then-collapse / fidelity gap):**\n  - **SG11 (`supergrok`)** — flat/collapse: `.audit_notes.md:153,170` (\"SG11: step fix WORKS (train 0.998 @ step400) but COLLAPSES to 0.014\").\n  - **SG15 (`supergrok15`)** — memorize-then-collapse: `.audit_notes.md:171-172` (\"memorize-then-COLLAPSE (trn 0.610@1500 → 0.014@2500). Inherent meta-net dynamics\").\n  - **SG2 (`supergrok2`)** — won't grok, CSA idx_UQ fidelity gap: `grokking_race_v2.py:1511-1513` (\"WON'T GROK… CSA lightning-indexer drops idx_UQ + scores /sqrt(rank) not /sqrt(d)… diverges for N&gt;64\"); also `tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py:296-309` (the N&gt;64 CSA fidelity PROBE) and `wiring_check.py:95-98`.\n  - **neuralgrok on L3** — kernel-ready but race-blocked (frozen-amplifier on L3): `wiring_check.py:115-123` (\"train_neuralgrok trains the amplifier BETWEEN steps via a differentiable Python rebuild… the L3 megakernel returns only the loss → frozen-amplifier (non-faithful) race\"). NOTE: neuralgrok groks via the **eager** race path (`.audit_notes.md:115`) but the L3-TC neuralgrok cell is not the faithful production path.\n  - Verdict summary: `.audit_notes.md:175-178` — \"8/11 grok. The 3 SuperGrok-family… are NUMERICALLY correct (parity 11/0) but their bilevel meta-net DYNAMICS destabilize post-memorization (SG11/15) or have CSA/HCA kernel-fidelity gaps (SG2).\"\n\n### Smaller/faster smoke variant (YES)\n**`/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/wiring_check.py`** — routes each cell through the real `train_&lt;opt&gt;` for **only `--steps 4`** (`:309`), no eval/no early-stop (`:172-173`), and asserts the **observed engine == wgmma** (`:240-258`). This is the fast per-cell path/parity smoke (~seconds/cell), the right pre-flight before the full race:\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\n# Smoke ONE cell:\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 wiring_check.py --opts supergrok2 --models decoder --steps 4\n# Smoke ALL 33 + fail-loud if any not L3-TC:\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 wiring_check.py --require-all 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/wiring.json.log\n# PASS signal per cell:  \"[PASS L3-TC] &lt;opt&gt;/&lt;model&gt;  path=L3-TC-megakernel(wgmma)\"\n# FAIL signal:           \"[BLOCKED   ] ...\"  or exit 1 under --require-all\ngrep -E \"\\[BLOCKED|FAIL-LOUD|ERROR \\(\" /tmp/wiring.json.log   # any hit = problem\n```\nOutput JSON: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/results/h100_grokking_race/wiring_check.json`. The single-step **correctness** smoke is the tail gate (Section 3c): `python -m tests.hw.test_l3tc_tail_gate --cell supergrok2/decoder`.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 — AUDIT FINDINGS\n\n### (a) \"Vaporware multistep gates\" for grokadamw &amp; prodigy\n**Location:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py`. The functions `_multistep_grokadamw_parity()` and `_prodigy_multistep_parity()` are **referenced in 8 comments** (lines **303-304, 313, 321, 332, 338-339**) as \"the load-bearing check… NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT\" — **but they are NEVER DEFINED and NEVER CALLED.** Confirmed:\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\ngrep -c \"def _multistep_grokadamw_parity\\|def _prodigy_multistep_parity\" tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py   # =&gt; 0\ngrep -n \"_multistep_grokadamw_parity\\|_prodigy_multistep_parity\" tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py            # =&gt; only comment lines\n```\n`run_cell_gate()` (`:737-1115`) runs only the **single-step** (1a/1b) + A/A/A (2) checks. The comments themselves admit the single-step gate is BLIND to the load-bearing mechanism: grokadamw's global grad-norm clip is inert at step 1 (‖g‖₂≈0.72&lt;1, `:302`) and only \"fires by ~step 50\"; prodigy's d-adaptation \"fires at step≥2\" (`:330,338`) so at step 1 `d` stays frozen at `d0=1e-6`.\n**What's missing:** the multi-step parity functions that the docstrings promise exist.\n**Minimal fix:** implement `_multistep_grokadamw_parity(model)` and `_prodigy_multistep_parity(model)` as ~50-step loops comparing kernel state vs the eager optimizer (for prodigy: assert kernel `d` tracks eager `_d_lr` and a `d`-frozen-at-`d0` control diverges, per the docstring at `:332-333`; for grokadamw: run past step 50 so the clip fires and assert kernel-vs-eager params match), then **call them from `run_cell_gate`** in the `opt in (\"grokadamw\",\"prodigy\")` branches and fold their verdict into `ok`. Until then these cells are registered on **single-step evidence only** — the multi-step \"honest registration\" the comments claim is vaporware.\n\n### (b) Single-source apply-call check is WARN-ONLY\n**File:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/check_math_single_source.py`. The apply-symbol check is at **lines 375-384** inside `check()`:\n```python\nsym = _FUSED_APPLY_SYMBOL.get(opt)\nif sym and sym not in fused:\n    _emit_warning(                      # &lt;-- line 377: WARNS instead of failing\n        f\"{FUSED_CONSUMER} #includes {_canonical_header(opt)} but \"\n        f\"does not appear to CALL its apply symbol `{sym}` ...\")\n```\n`_emit_warning` (`:263-268`) writes `[WARN]` to stderr and **returns** — it does NOT append to `failures`, so `main()` (`:450`) still exits 0. The `#include` presence IS hard-failed (`:365-369` appends to `failures`), but a **dead include with no call** only warns.\n**Fix to fail-hard:** change `:377` from `_emit_warning(...)` to `failures.append(f\"[structural] {FUSED_CONSUMER} #includes {_canonical_header(opt)} but does NOT call its apply symbol `{sym}` — dead include / wrong path for '{opt}'.\")`. (The comment at `:381-384` explicitly flags this as a deliberate softening \"TODO: harden to a hard-fail post-refactor\" because of an in-flight `opt_components.cuh` rename — so confirm the rename has landed before flipping it.) NOTE: SG2 is intentionally exempt via `SG2_SEPARATE_PATH` (`:260,352-364`) — it routes through `launch_csa_hca` not `opt_components.cuh`, so leave its WARN.\n\n### (c) \"HW gate not in CI\"\n**The gate:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py` (pytest marker `@pytest.mark.hw`, `:1130`) — and the SG2 sub-gate `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py`, plus the path-smoke `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/wiring_check.py`. **None run in CI.**\n**Where CI is defined:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/.github/workflows/ci.yml`. The pytest job (`pytest_cpu`, **`:1301-1316`**) runs `pytest tests/ -m \"not hw\"` (`:1316`) — i.e. it **explicitly excludes** the hw gate. CI today runs: `check_math_single_source.py` (`:1211-1212`), `verify_all` phases 1/4/5 (`:1224-1250`), `profile_maximal` tiers C/D/E (`:1263-1291`) — all **no-GPU**. The `-m hw` tail gate / `wiring_check.py --require-all` / `_sg2_l3tc_gate` need a GPU runner and are **not wired**.\n**To wire it in:** add a GPU-runner job to `ci.yml` (mirroring the `pytest_cpu` block structure) that runs `PYTHONPATH=. python3 -m pytest tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py -m hw -q` (+ optionally `python3 wiring_check.py --require-all`). It must target a self-hosted/GPU runner (the existing jobs are `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`, CPU-only, which is why the gate was excluded). The gate self-skips cleanly off-GPU (`:1131` `skipif(not _gpu_ready())`), so it's safe to add even on a CPU runner — it just won't exercise anything there.\n\n### (d) supergrok2/vit TEST-STATE-LEAKAGE\n**File:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py`. The assert at **`:154-155`** (`k_sharp.abs().max() &gt; 1e-8`, \"kernel sharpness ~0 — the in-kernel SAM 2nd backward did not run\") is the liveness tripwire. The leaking state is documented at **`:198-203`**: the per-op oracle `ops.supergrok2_batched_step` (the ~15-20-launch path, called in `_per_op_oracle_step` `:87-114`) **\"perturbs a device-global the SUBSEQUENT TC SG2 launch reads → NaN\"** — same class as the neuralgrok per-op `__constant__`/scratch contamination at `test_l3tc_tail_gate.py:179` and `:1042-1049` (prior L3 launch's raw-cudaMalloc scratch teardown perturbs `__constant__`/shared staging). So when the suite runs 33 cells **back-to-back in one process** (the `@pytest.mark.parametrize` over `_CELLS` at `test_l3tc_tail_gate.py:1132`), a prior cell's per-op launch leaves a **device-global** (CUDA `__constant__`/global scratch) dirty, and the next SG2 TC launch reads garbage → sharpness collapses to ~0 → the `:154` assert fires (or NaN).\n**The per-cell isolation fix already partially present** (`:204-218`, the `_one()` helper): rebuild a fresh model per run via `_build(model)` (new `g3,c3,m3,data3`), `torch.cuda.synchronize()` + `del m3,opt3,cc,g3,data3` + **`torch.cuda.empty_cache()`** between runs (`:215-217`), AND **ordering**: run A/A/A on the clean TC path **BEFORE** the B1 per-op oracle (`:196-203,232-234`), because the oracle is what contaminates. \n**To fully isolate back-to-back CELLS** (not just the 3 A/A/A runs within one cell): the missing piece is a **per-cell teardown** that zeroes the device-global the per-op kernel dirties. Options, cheapest first: (1) a pytest fixture with `autouse=True` that calls `torch.cuda.synchronize(); torch.cuda.empty_cache()` after each cell (insufficient alone — empty_cache doesn't reset `__constant__`); (2) **module reload** of `grokking_optimizers._ops` between contaminating cells to re-zero `__constant__` (heavy); (3) the correct fix — **run the SG2 gate (and neuralgrok) in a SUBPROCESS per cell** so the device-global never crosses cells, OR have the kernel re-stage/zero its `__constant__` scratch at launch entry (the real kernel-side fix, out of test scope). The test-side fix the directive wants is **#3 (subprocess-per-cell isolation)** or an explicit state-zero of the contaminated global between cells; the current empty_cache+del+ordering only protects within a single cell's 3 A/A/A runs.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 — ORDERING + GPU BUDGET\n\n### Recommended order (cheapest / read-only-on-binary first)\n| # | Step | GPU? | Why first | Approx cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `check_math_single_source.py` | No | pure file-hash, instant; catches math drift from the rebuild | &lt;5 s |\n| 2 | `profile_maximal --tier C/D/E` | No | gfx942 ISA + functional descent + TPU HLO; no nvcc | ~1-2 min |\n| 3 | `profile_maximal --tier A/B` (SASS) | No (nvcc host) | compile+cuobjdump the 4 model TUs + 4-cell sample; **read-only on binary**, no kernel launch | ~25 min (compile-heavy) |\n| 4 | `wiring_check.py --require-all` (smoke) | **Yes** | 4-step route per cell; proves all 33 take wgmma L3-TC before any expensive test | ~33 cells × few s ≈ 3-5 min |\n| 5 | `pytest tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py -m hw` | **Yes** | single-step + A/A/A per-cell correctness (33 cells, runs B1/oracle, the SG2 leakage-sensitive path) | ~33 cells × ~30-60 s ≈ 20-35 min |\n| 6 | full grokking race (per-cell, multi-step) | **Yes** | the slowest; only the GROK/COLLAPSE functional outcome | ~3-15 GPU-min/cell |\n\nSteps 1-3 are **read-only-on-binary** (host compile/inspect, no GPU kernel) — run them while the GPU is busy. Steps 4-6 **need the GPU**.\n\n### GPU-minutes for the whole gauntlet (33 cells)\n- Smoke (step 4): **~3-5 GPU-min total** (all 33, 4 steps each).\n- Tail gate (step 5): **~20-35 GPU-min total** (33 cells; SG2 cells heaviest due to the per-op oracle).\n- Full race (step 6): **the dominant cost.** 33 cells × ~3-15 min = **~2-8 GPU-hours** at 15k steps/seed/cell, 1 seed. With the documented 5 seeds (`:2630`) it is 5× that — for verification, **run 1 seed/cell**.\n- **Total realistic verification budget: ~3-9 GPU-hours** (dominated by the race), or **~30-45 GPU-min** if you stop after the smoke + tail gate (steps 4-5) and only race the cells you specifically need GROK evidence for.\n\n### What can batch\n- **Steps 1-3 batch entirely off-GPU** — run concurrently with anything on the GPU.\n- **Step 4 (wiring_check)** already loops all 33 in one process and caches data/init per model (`wiring_check.py:159-188`) — single invocation.\n- **Step 5 (tail gate)** runs all 33 via one pytest parametrize — single invocation (**but see Section 3d: the SG2/neuralgrok cells need subprocess isolation or they'll cross-contaminate in that single process** — consider `pytest --forked` or running `supergrok2/*` + `neuralgrok/*` cells in separate invocations).\n- **Step 6 (race)** does NOT batch cleanly — run per-cell (or use `--gpus 0,1,2,3` at `:2499` to parallelize cells across GPUs, one optimizer per GPU). Skip the 4 documented-won't-grok cells (SG11/SG15/SG2, and neuralgrok-on-L3) for grok evidence — verify those via the **single-step tail gate + the N&gt;64 CSA probe** instead, since their collapse is the documented expected result, not a regression.\n\n**One-line gate greps for a clean run:**\n```bash\ngrep -E \"VERDICT: PASS\" /tmp/pmax*.log                                   # §1 maximal\ngrep -E \"\\[BLOCKED|FAIL-LOUD|ERROR \\(\" /tmp/wiring.json.log || echo OK   # §2 smoke: all L3-TC\ngrep -E \"[0-9]+ passed.* 0 failed|FAILED\" /tmp/tailgate.log              # §3 tail gate\ngrep -E \"WIRING-GUARD.*DEGRADED|nan|trn=0\\.01[0-4].*tl=4\\.5\" /tmp/race_*.log  # §2 race: bugs\n```\n\n**Key file references:** profile: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_optimizers/profile_maximal.py` (cubin via `cuobjdump -sass` at `:285`, spill regex `:255`, HGMMA regex `:287`); race: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_race_v2.py` (CLI `:2493`, won't-grok doc SG2 `:1511`); smoke: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/wiring_check.py`; gates: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py` (vaporware refs `:303-339`, no defs), `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py` (leak `:198-203`, assert `:154`); single-source `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/check_math_single_source.py:377`; CI `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/.github/workflows/ci.yml:1316` (excludes `-m hw`); audit substrate `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/.audit_notes.md:113-178`; PTX fast-math `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/STAGE3_PTX_AUDIT.md`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>146589</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>32</tool_uses><duration_ms>274111</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
2008:{"parentUuid":"5c768b86-3dd2-4bec-aa28-3ebcabb65410","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"queued_command","prompt":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9583582925c26324</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01J3jox4iZtSvxhRtEdSoJHv</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-0/-/80b51e31-b580-43b6-8244-fd7633ee1338/tasks/a9583582925c26324.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Scope verification gauntlet (read-only)\" completed</summary>\n<result>I have everything. The `redux.sync` is emitted as inline PTX asm in `primitives.cuh:196` (becomes `REDUX` in SASS, but profile_maximal does NOT grep it). All four audit findings, the maximality check, the race harness, and the ordering/budget are fully mapped. Let me compile the surgical execution plan.\n\n---\n\n# VERIFICATION GAUNTLET — EXECUTION PLAN (33 L3-TC wgmma cells)\n\nAll paths absolute. Run after the builder's rebuild lands and `import grokking_optimizers._ops` succeeds. Cells = 11 opts × 3 models {decoder, vit, mamba}.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1 — MAX-PTX maximality check\n\n**Script:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_optimizers/profile_maximal.py`\n\n**CRITICAL CORRECTION up front:** this tool audits **SASS** (disassembled cubin), **not PTX**, and it does **NOT** profile the 33 mega cells individually. It compiles **4 GEMM-bearing model TUs** (Tier A) + a **4-cell sample** (Tier B), because the \"maximal\" decision is in the compute-bound model GEMMs — the 33 element-local optimizer tails are scalar by design (`profile_maximal.py:303-312`, `:366-370`). There is no per-cell-×33 loop in this tool.\n\n### Invocations\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\n# Full SASS audit (Tiers A+B need nvcc+cuobjdump; ~25 min, compile-heavy):\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 -m grokking_optimizers.profile_maximal 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/pmax.log\n# Tier A only (the WGMMA/TMA/spill maximality decision, 4 model TUs):\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 -m grokking_optimizers.profile_maximal --tier A 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/pmax_A.log\n# Tier B only (ptxas regs/smem/spills on a 4-cell sample + 3 opt kernels):\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 -m grokking_optimizers.profile_maximal --tier B 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/pmax_B.log\n```\nNo flag selects an arbitrary cell. The Tier-B sample is hardcoded at `profile_maximal.py:367-371`: `mamba3/adamw, mamba3/supergrok2, transformer_decoder/muon, vit/prodigy` + opt kernels `launch_adamw, launch_supergrok2, launch_muon`. To audit a different cell's SASS you must edit `_CELL_SAMPLE` (you said read-only — so accept this sample as the representative set, OR run the per-cell wgmma proof through `wiring_check.py` in Section 2 instead, which observes the live engine).\n\n### How it gets the cubin (your Q)\n`_nvcc_profile()` at `profile_maximal.py:258-296`: it compiles the `.cu` itself with `nvcc -o &lt;tmp&gt;.o` (`:261-262`, flags `_MAX_NVCC` at `:153-158` = `-O3 --use_fast_math -DNDEBUG arch=compute_90a,code=sm_90a`, plus `-Xptxas -v`), then runs **`cuobjdump -sass &lt;tmp&gt;.o`** (`:285`). So: **SASS via cuobjdump**, on a freshly-compiled object — **not** nvdisasm, **not** PTX, **not** a prebuilt cubin.\n\n### PASS criterion (literal strings to grep in the log)\nPer-TU PASS gate is `pr.wgmma&gt;0 and pr.tma&gt;0 and pr.c7509==0 and pr.unexpected_spill_bytes==0` (`:337-338`). In the printed log:\n\n| Check | PASS signal | FAIL signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Overall TU | `PASS  [A] &lt;label&gt;` | `FAIL  [A] &lt;label&gt;` |\n| (a) WGMMA present | `WGMMA=&lt;N&gt;` with N&gt;0 in the detail line | `← NO tensor-core MMA (not maximal!)` (`:343`) |\n| (a) TMA present | `TMA=&lt;N&gt;` with N&gt;0 | (TMA=0 → overall FAIL, no dedicated banner) |\n| not serialized | `C7509=0` | `← wgmma SERIALIZED (not maximal!)` (`:345`) |\n| (b) zero spills | `spills=0B` (or `spills=8B` **with** the dead-code note) | `← register SPILLS (not maximal!)` (`:347`) |\n| Final verdict | `VERDICT: PASS` (`:715`) | `VERDICT: FAIL — &lt;n&gt; probe(s) not maximal` (`:720`) |\n\n```bash\ngrep -E \"FAIL |VERDICT: FAIL|not maximal!|SERIALIZED\" /tmp/pmax_A.log   # any hit = fail\ngrep -E \"PASS .*\\[A\\]|VERDICT: PASS|WGMMA=[1-9]\" /tmp/pmax_A.log         # pass evidence\n```\n\n**The spill marker (your Q):** the literal ptxas string is **`bytes spill stores`** (regex `profile_maximal.py:255,268`). The per-kernel block is `Function properties for &lt;MANGLED&gt; ... N bytes stack frame, M bytes spill stores`. There is **exactly ONE allowlisted 8-byte spiller** — the dead-code CUTLASS TF32-RS GEMM `MMA_64x128x8_F32TF32TF32_RS_TN` (runtime-unreachable via `vit_run_gemm_atb&lt;float&gt;` scalar fallback; allowlist tokens at `:198-202`). So **`spills=8B` is still a PASS** iff the detail line also shows `ALLOWED: known dead-code CUTLASS TF32-RS GEMM`. Any OTHER spiller → `unexpected_spill_bytes&gt;0` → FAIL. This is confirmed live in `.audit_notes.md:84-90` (6P/5F where all 5 fails were this single 8B dead kernel).\n\n### (a) WGMMA + TMA, (c) fast-math — IMPORTANT naming gotcha\n- **WGMMA in SASS is `HGMMA`/`GMMA`**, not the literal \"wgmma\". The regex is `\\b[HUW]?GMMA\\b` (`:287`). **`grep wgmma` returns 0 = false-negative** — `.audit_notes.md:87` confirms: \"grep 'wgmma'=0 is false-neg (SASS mnemonic=HGMMA). 256 HGMMA + 622 TMA(UTMALDG)\". Grep the cubin for `HGMMA` and `UTMALDG`, not `wgmma`/`TMA`.\n- **TMA** = `UTMALDG|UBLKCP|cp.async.bulk` (`:288`).\n- **(c) inlined fast-math** — profile_maximal does **NOT** grep `rsqrt.approx`/`ex2.approx`/`fma.rn`/`redux.sync`. Those are **PTX**-level strings; at SASS level they lower to **`MUFU`** (rsqrt/ex2 → `MUFU.RSQ`/`MUFU.EX2`) and **`FFMA`** (fma.rn), which the tool *does* count and print as `MUFU=&lt;N&gt;` (`:290,339`). The PTX fast-math proof lives separately in **`/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/STAGE3_PTX_AUDIT.md`** (documents `ex2.approx.f32`:12,43; `rsqrt.approx.f32`:13,195; `fma.rn.f32`:158,211). `redux.sync.add.u32` is hand-written inline PTX asm at **`/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/csrc/backends/cuda/sm_90/primitives.cuh:196`** (not grepped by profile_maximal). So to check (c) you grep two different surfaces:\n```bash\n# SASS form (what profile_maximal prints): expect MUFU&gt;0 and FFMA&gt;0\ngrep -E \"MUFU=[1-9]|FFMA\" /tmp/pmax_A.log\n# raw PTX fast-math (if you compile -ptx yourself): the literal approx ops\n# documented in scripts/STAGE3_PTX_AUDIT.md — NOT produced by this tool\n```\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2 — GROKKING RACE (multi-step functional test)\n\n**Harness:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_race_v2.py` (CLI: `:2493`). Selection: `--optimizers &lt;key&gt; --tasks &lt;model&gt;`; budget: `--early-stop-max-steps`; seeds: `--num-seeds`/`--seeds`.\n\n**KEY ARCHITECTURE FACT** (`.audit_notes.md:99-103`): the race runs **fused-OPTIMIZER kernels × EAGER PyTorch models**. The L3-TC megakernel path is taken inside `train_&lt;opt&gt;` via `_try_fused_train_step(mtype, opt, …)` (e.g. SG2 at `grokking_race_v2.py:1519`; SG11/15 at `:1259`/`:1385`; neuralgrok at `:1151`). To force the wgmma L3-TC engine you must set `matmul_precision=bf16` (the default) and `use_fused=True` (the default). **Also note `.audit_notes.md:101`: race `adamw` uses stock `torch.optim.AdamW(fused=True)`, NOT the pkg adamw kernel** — so adamw is not a kernel test.\n\n### Single-cell command (one optimizer × one model, L3-TC bf16)\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 grokking_race_v2.py \\\n  --optimizers supergrok2 --tasks decoder \\\n  --num-seeds 1 --train-test-ratios 50 \\\n  --early-stop-max-steps 15000 --eval-every 10 \\\n  --no-status-server 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/race_sg2_decoder.log\n```\n- Optimizer keys (`:51`, `wiring_check.py:51-52`): `adamw neuralgrok grokadamw supergrok supergrok15 supergrok2 grokfast muon lion looksam prodigy` (note SG11's race key is **`supergrok`**, not `supergrok11`).\n- Models: `decoder vit mamba`.\n- **GPU-time budget:** the documented decoder reference run was **15k steps** (`.audit_notes.md:113`, \"decoder 50/50 15k\"). A grokking cell crosses val≥0.95 and stops via patience (50 evals × eval_every 10 = 500-step hold, `:264-265`); a DNF runs the full `early_stop_max_steps`. Rough wall: decoder ~3-6 GPU-min/cell at 15k on H100; vit (MNIST, larger) ~2-3×; mamba (python-fallback scan, `.audit_notes.md:103`) slowest. Budget **15k steps; cap 20k** (the argparse default `:2511`).\n\n### Verify it actually took L3-TC (don't trust silently-degraded runs)\nThe train loop stamps `train_path` via the WIRING-GUARD (`grokking_race_v2.py:738-783`). A degrade prints to **stderr**:\n```bash\ngrep -E \"\\[WIRING-GUARD\\].*DEGRADED|expected .* but ran\" /tmp/race_sg2_decoder.log   # must be EMPTY\n```\nFor a pure path check without a full race, use the smoke variant (Section below).\n\n### GROK vs COLLAPSE vs HIDDEN-BUG signals (grep the log / result JSON)\nResults land in `results/&lt;output&gt;/...json` (per `TrainResult`, `:567-610`). The tqdm postfix prints `trn=… val=… tst=… tl=…` every eval (`:718`).\n\n| Outcome | Signal to grep |\n|---|---|\n| **GROK** | `val` and `tst` climb to ~`1.0` after a delay; result JSON `grokking_step_test_confirmed: true` + `final_test_acc` ≈ 1.0 + `stopping_reason: \"val_acc_threshold\"` (`:533`). |\n| **COLLAPSE (memorize-then-collapse)** | `trn` rises (e.g. 0.6-1.0) then **drops back** while `tst` stays ~chance; final `final_test_acc` ≈ `0.01` with a non-trivial `best_metric_acc` earlier. Documented pattern: `.audit_notes.md:153,171-172`. |\n| **HIDDEN BUG** | `tl=nan`/`tl=4.577` flat at `trn≈0.014` (chance = 1/97 ≈ 0.0103) for the whole run = NaN / no-learning; `component_failures` non-empty in JSON (`:606`) = a side-component (sam/meta/bilevel) silently broke; or a `[WIRING-GUARD] DEGRADED` line = parity/path drift. |\n\n```bash\ngrep -E \"nan|trn=0\\.01[0-4].*tl=4\\.5|component_failures|WIRING-GUARD\" /tmp/race_*.log\n```\n\n### EXPECTED to grok vs documented-WON'T-grok (with file:line citations)\nFrom the live run-1 result (`.audit_notes.md:113-118`) and the post-fix integration (`:168-178`):\n- **EXPECTED to GROK (8/11):** adamw, lion, grokfast, grokadamw, looksam, neuralgrok, muon, **prodigy** (prodigy now groks post-EMA-fix: `.audit_notes.md:169` \"Prodigy: GROKS @ step 1000\").\n- **Documented WON'T grok (memorize-then-collapse / fidelity gap):**\n  - **SG11 (`supergrok`)** — flat/collapse: `.audit_notes.md:153,170` (\"SG11: step fix WORKS (train 0.998 @ step400) but COLLAPSES to 0.014\").\n  - **SG15 (`supergrok15`)** — memorize-then-collapse: `.audit_notes.md:171-172` (\"memorize-then-COLLAPSE (trn 0.610@1500 → 0.014@2500). Inherent meta-net dynamics\").\n  - **SG2 (`supergrok2`)** — won't grok, CSA idx_UQ fidelity gap: `grokking_race_v2.py:1511-1513` (\"WON'T GROK… CSA lightning-indexer drops idx_UQ + scores /sqrt(rank) not /sqrt(d)… diverges for N&gt;64\"); also `tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py:296-309` (the N&gt;64 CSA fidelity PROBE) and `wiring_check.py:95-98`.\n  - **neuralgrok on L3** — kernel-ready but race-blocked (frozen-amplifier on L3): `wiring_check.py:115-123` (\"train_neuralgrok trains the amplifier BETWEEN steps via a differentiable Python rebuild… the L3 megakernel returns only the loss → frozen-amplifier (non-faithful) race\"). NOTE: neuralgrok groks via the **eager** race path (`.audit_notes.md:115`) but the L3-TC neuralgrok cell is not the faithful production path.\n  - Verdict summary: `.audit_notes.md:175-178` — \"8/11 grok. The 3 SuperGrok-family… are NUMERICALLY correct (parity 11/0) but their bilevel meta-net DYNAMICS destabilize post-memorization (SG11/15) or have CSA/HCA kernel-fidelity gaps (SG2).\"\n\n### Smaller/faster smoke variant (YES)\n**`/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/wiring_check.py`** — routes each cell through the real `train_&lt;opt&gt;` for **only `--steps 4`** (`:309`), no eval/no early-stop (`:172-173`), and asserts the **observed engine == wgmma** (`:240-258`). This is the fast per-cell path/parity smoke (~seconds/cell), the right pre-flight before the full race:\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\n# Smoke ONE cell:\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 wiring_check.py --opts supergrok2 --models decoder --steps 4\n# Smoke ALL 33 + fail-loud if any not L3-TC:\nPYTHONPATH=. python3 wiring_check.py --require-all 2&gt;&amp;1 | tee /tmp/wiring.json.log\n# PASS signal per cell:  \"[PASS L3-TC] &lt;opt&gt;/&lt;model&gt;  path=L3-TC-megakernel(wgmma)\"\n# FAIL signal:           \"[BLOCKED   ] ...\"  or exit 1 under --require-all\ngrep -E \"\\[BLOCKED|FAIL-LOUD|ERROR \\(\" /tmp/wiring.json.log   # any hit = problem\n```\nOutput JSON: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/results/h100_grokking_race/wiring_check.json`. The single-step **correctness** smoke is the tail gate (Section 3c): `python -m tests.hw.test_l3tc_tail_gate --cell supergrok2/decoder`.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3 — AUDIT FINDINGS\n\n### (a) \"Vaporware multistep gates\" for grokadamw &amp; prodigy\n**Location:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py`. The functions `_multistep_grokadamw_parity()` and `_prodigy_multistep_parity()` are **referenced in 8 comments** (lines **303-304, 313, 321, 332, 338-339**) as \"the load-bearing check… NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT\" — **but they are NEVER DEFINED and NEVER CALLED.** Confirmed:\n```bash\ncd /workspace/SuperGrok1.5\ngrep -c \"def _multistep_grokadamw_parity\\|def _prodigy_multistep_parity\" tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py   # =&gt; 0\ngrep -n \"_multistep_grokadamw_parity\\|_prodigy_multistep_parity\" tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py            # =&gt; only comment lines\n```\n`run_cell_gate()` (`:737-1115`) runs only the **single-step** (1a/1b) + A/A/A (2) checks. The comments themselves admit the single-step gate is BLIND to the load-bearing mechanism: grokadamw's global grad-norm clip is inert at step 1 (‖g‖₂≈0.72&lt;1, `:302`) and only \"fires by ~step 50\"; prodigy's d-adaptation \"fires at step≥2\" (`:330,338`) so at step 1 `d` stays frozen at `d0=1e-6`.\n**What's missing:** the multi-step parity functions that the docstrings promise exist.\n**Minimal fix:** implement `_multistep_grokadamw_parity(model)` and `_prodigy_multistep_parity(model)` as ~50-step loops comparing kernel state vs the eager optimizer (for prodigy: assert kernel `d` tracks eager `_d_lr` and a `d`-frozen-at-`d0` control diverges, per the docstring at `:332-333`; for grokadamw: run past step 50 so the clip fires and assert kernel-vs-eager params match), then **call them from `run_cell_gate`** in the `opt in (\"grokadamw\",\"prodigy\")` branches and fold their verdict into `ok`. Until then these cells are registered on **single-step evidence only** — the multi-step \"honest registration\" the comments claim is vaporware.\n\n### (b) Single-source apply-call check is WARN-ONLY\n**File:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/check_math_single_source.py`. The apply-symbol check is at **lines 375-384** inside `check()`:\n```python\nsym = _FUSED_APPLY_SYMBOL.get(opt)\nif sym and sym not in fused:\n    _emit_warning(                      # &lt;-- line 377: WARNS instead of failing\n        f\"{FUSED_CONSUMER} #includes {_canonical_header(opt)} but \"\n        f\"does not appear to CALL its apply symbol `{sym}` ...\")\n```\n`_emit_warning` (`:263-268`) writes `[WARN]` to stderr and **returns** — it does NOT append to `failures`, so `main()` (`:450`) still exits 0. The `#include` presence IS hard-failed (`:365-369` appends to `failures`), but a **dead include with no call** only warns.\n**Fix to fail-hard:** change `:377` from `_emit_warning(...)` to `failures.append(f\"[structural] {FUSED_CONSUMER} #includes {_canonical_header(opt)} but does NOT call its apply symbol `{sym}` — dead include / wrong path for '{opt}'.\")`. (The comment at `:381-384` explicitly flags this as a deliberate softening \"TODO: harden to a hard-fail post-refactor\" because of an in-flight `opt_components.cuh` rename — so confirm the rename has landed before flipping it.) NOTE: SG2 is intentionally exempt via `SG2_SEPARATE_PATH` (`:260,352-364`) — it routes through `launch_csa_hca` not `opt_components.cuh`, so leave its WARN.\n\n### (c) \"HW gate not in CI\"\n**The gate:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py` (pytest marker `@pytest.mark.hw`, `:1130`) — and the SG2 sub-gate `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py`, plus the path-smoke `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/wiring_check.py`. **None run in CI.**\n**Where CI is defined:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/.github/workflows/ci.yml`. The pytest job (`pytest_cpu`, **`:1301-1316`**) runs `pytest tests/ -m \"not hw\"` (`:1316`) — i.e. it **explicitly excludes** the hw gate. CI today runs: `check_math_single_source.py` (`:1211-1212`), `verify_all` phases 1/4/5 (`:1224-1250`), `profile_maximal` tiers C/D/E (`:1263-1291`) — all **no-GPU**. The `-m hw` tail gate / `wiring_check.py --require-all` / `_sg2_l3tc_gate` need a GPU runner and are **not wired**.\n**To wire it in:** add a GPU-runner job to `ci.yml` (mirroring the `pytest_cpu` block structure) that runs `PYTHONPATH=. python3 -m pytest tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py -m hw -q` (+ optionally `python3 wiring_check.py --require-all`). It must target a self-hosted/GPU runner (the existing jobs are `runs-on: ubuntu-latest`, CPU-only, which is why the gate was excluded). The gate self-skips cleanly off-GPU (`:1131` `skipif(not _gpu_ready())`), so it's safe to add even on a CPU runner — it just won't exercise anything there.\n\n### (d) supergrok2/vit TEST-STATE-LEAKAGE\n**File:** `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py`. The assert at **`:154-155`** (`k_sharp.abs().max() &gt; 1e-8`, \"kernel sharpness ~0 — the in-kernel SAM 2nd backward did not run\") is the liveness tripwire. The leaking state is documented at **`:198-203`**: the per-op oracle `ops.supergrok2_batched_step` (the ~15-20-launch path, called in `_per_op_oracle_step` `:87-114`) **\"perturbs a device-global the SUBSEQUENT TC SG2 launch reads → NaN\"** — same class as the neuralgrok per-op `__constant__`/scratch contamination at `test_l3tc_tail_gate.py:179` and `:1042-1049` (prior L3 launch's raw-cudaMalloc scratch teardown perturbs `__constant__`/shared staging). So when the suite runs 33 cells **back-to-back in one process** (the `@pytest.mark.parametrize` over `_CELLS` at `test_l3tc_tail_gate.py:1132`), a prior cell's per-op launch leaves a **device-global** (CUDA `__constant__`/global scratch) dirty, and the next SG2 TC launch reads garbage → sharpness collapses to ~0 → the `:154` assert fires (or NaN).\n**The per-cell isolation fix already partially present** (`:204-218`, the `_one()` helper): rebuild a fresh model per run via `_build(model)` (new `g3,c3,m3,data3`), `torch.cuda.synchronize()` + `del m3,opt3,cc,g3,data3` + **`torch.cuda.empty_cache()`** between runs (`:215-217`), AND **ordering**: run A/A/A on the clean TC path **BEFORE** the B1 per-op oracle (`:196-203,232-234`), because the oracle is what contaminates. \n**To fully isolate back-to-back CELLS** (not just the 3 A/A/A runs within one cell): the missing piece is a **per-cell teardown** that zeroes the device-global the per-op kernel dirties. Options, cheapest first: (1) a pytest fixture with `autouse=True` that calls `torch.cuda.synchronize(); torch.cuda.empty_cache()` after each cell (insufficient alone — empty_cache doesn't reset `__constant__`); (2) **module reload** of `grokking_optimizers._ops` between contaminating cells to re-zero `__constant__` (heavy); (3) the correct fix — **run the SG2 gate (and neuralgrok) in a SUBPROCESS per cell** so the device-global never crosses cells, OR have the kernel re-stage/zero its `__constant__` scratch at launch entry (the real kernel-side fix, out of test scope). The test-side fix the directive wants is **#3 (subprocess-per-cell isolation)** or an explicit state-zero of the contaminated global between cells; the current empty_cache+del+ordering only protects within a single cell's 3 A/A/A runs.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 4 — ORDERING + GPU BUDGET\n\n### Recommended order (cheapest / read-only-on-binary first)\n| # | Step | GPU? | Why first | Approx cost |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `check_math_single_source.py` | No | pure file-hash, instant; catches math drift from the rebuild | &lt;5 s |\n| 2 | `profile_maximal --tier C/D/E` | No | gfx942 ISA + functional descent + TPU HLO; no nvcc | ~1-2 min |\n| 3 | `profile_maximal --tier A/B` (SASS) | No (nvcc host) | compile+cuobjdump the 4 model TUs + 4-cell sample; **read-only on binary**, no kernel launch | ~25 min (compile-heavy) |\n| 4 | `wiring_check.py --require-all` (smoke) | **Yes** | 4-step route per cell; proves all 33 take wgmma L3-TC before any expensive test | ~33 cells × few s ≈ 3-5 min |\n| 5 | `pytest tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py -m hw` | **Yes** | single-step + A/A/A per-cell correctness (33 cells, runs B1/oracle, the SG2 leakage-sensitive path) | ~33 cells × ~30-60 s ≈ 20-35 min |\n| 6 | full grokking race (per-cell, multi-step) | **Yes** | the slowest; only the GROK/COLLAPSE functional outcome | ~3-15 GPU-min/cell |\n\nSteps 1-3 are **read-only-on-binary** (host compile/inspect, no GPU kernel) — run them while the GPU is busy. Steps 4-6 **need the GPU**.\n\n### GPU-minutes for the whole gauntlet (33 cells)\n- Smoke (step 4): **~3-5 GPU-min total** (all 33, 4 steps each).\n- Tail gate (step 5): **~20-35 GPU-min total** (33 cells; SG2 cells heaviest due to the per-op oracle).\n- Full race (step 6): **the dominant cost.** 33 cells × ~3-15 min = **~2-8 GPU-hours** at 15k steps/seed/cell, 1 seed. With the documented 5 seeds (`:2630`) it is 5× that — for verification, **run 1 seed/cell**.\n- **Total realistic verification budget: ~3-9 GPU-hours** (dominated by the race), or **~30-45 GPU-min** if you stop after the smoke + tail gate (steps 4-5) and only race the cells you specifically need GROK evidence for.\n\n### What can batch\n- **Steps 1-3 batch entirely off-GPU** — run concurrently with anything on the GPU.\n- **Step 4 (wiring_check)** already loops all 33 in one process and caches data/init per model (`wiring_check.py:159-188`) — single invocation.\n- **Step 5 (tail gate)** runs all 33 via one pytest parametrize — single invocation (**but see Section 3d: the SG2/neuralgrok cells need subprocess isolation or they'll cross-contaminate in that single process** — consider `pytest --forked` or running `supergrok2/*` + `neuralgrok/*` cells in separate invocations).\n- **Step 6 (race)** does NOT batch cleanly — run per-cell (or use `--gpus 0,1,2,3` at `:2499` to parallelize cells across GPUs, one optimizer per GPU). Skip the 4 documented-won't-grok cells (SG11/SG15/SG2, and neuralgrok-on-L3) for grok evidence — verify those via the **single-step tail gate + the N&gt;64 CSA probe** instead, since their collapse is the documented expected result, not a regression.\n\n**One-line gate greps for a clean run:**\n```bash\ngrep -E \"VERDICT: PASS\" /tmp/pmax*.log                                   # §1 maximal\ngrep -E \"\\[BLOCKED|FAIL-LOUD|ERROR \\(\" /tmp/wiring.json.log || echo OK   # §2 smoke: all L3-TC\ngrep -E \"[0-9]+ passed.* 0 failed|FAILED\" /tmp/tailgate.log              # §3 tail gate\ngrep -E \"WIRING-GUARD.*DEGRADED|nan|trn=0\\.01[0-4].*tl=4\\.5\" /tmp/race_*.log  # §2 race: bugs\n```\n\n**Key file references:** profile: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_optimizers/profile_maximal.py` (cubin via `cuobjdump -sass` at `:285`, spill regex `:255`, HGMMA regex `:287`); race: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/grokking_race_v2.py` (CLI `:2493`, won't-grok doc SG2 `:1511`); smoke: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/wiring_check.py`; gates: `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/test_l3tc_tail_gate.py` (vaporware refs `:303-339`, no defs), `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/tests/hw/_sg2_l3tc_gate.py` (leak `:198-203`, assert `:154`); single-source `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/check_math_single_source.py:377`; CI `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/.github/workflows/ci.yml:1316` (excludes `-m hw`); audit substrate `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/.audit_notes.md:113-178`; PTX fast-math `/workspace/SuperGrok1.5/scripts/STAGE3_PTX_AUDIT.md`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>146589</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>32</tool_uses><duration_ms>274111</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>","commandMode":"task-notification"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b25d2b0c-6924-4a9c-8625-add17c523e91","timestamp":"2026-06-12T00:13:43.436Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/workspace/SuperGrok1.5","sessionId":"80b51e31-b580-43b6-8244-fd7633ee1338","version":"2.1.173","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"sprightly-pondering-dewdrop"}
